<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andújar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Andujar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andújar is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Guadalquivir</a> in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaen">Jaén</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. In the 1220s it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-of-castile">Ferdinand III of Castile</a> as one of the castles handed over by his ally, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> prince al-Bayyasi, and it became a Castilian frontier town. It is known for its pottery and for the shrine of the Virgen de la Cabeza in the nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-morena">Sierra Morena</a>, a major pilgrimage centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andujar.jpg" alt="Andújar" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mileurista · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ayuntamiento_de_And%C3%BAjar.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
